PER-ENDPOINT DETAIL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. POST /v1/audio/transcriptions, model gpt-4o-mini-transcribe
   multipart/form-data, file = one-second.wav (32044 bytes, audio/wav)
   200 in all four cells; res.json() readable; body 149 bytes.
   Body shape (keys only):
     { text: string,
       usage: { type: string, total_tokens: number, input_tokens: number,
                input_token_details: { text_tokens: number,
                                       audio_tokens: number },
                output_tokens: number } }
   TRANSCRIPT RETURNED: the empty string "" -- identical in all four cells.
   The fixture is a pure 440 Hz sine with no speech, so an empty transcript is
   the correct answer and confirms the audio was accepted and processed rather
   than rejected. The CORS question is settled by the 200 and the readable JSON;
   the transcript content is incidental.

2a. POST /v1/chat/completions, model gpt-4o-mini, max_tokens 5,
    messages [{role:user, content:"Say hi"}]
   200 in all four cells; res.json() readable; body 820-824 bytes.
   Body shape (keys only):
     { id, object, created, model, choices[], usage{ prompt_tokens,
       completion_tokens, total_tokens, prompt_tokens_details{...},
       completion_tokens_details{...} }, service_tier, system_fingerprint }
   usage: prompt 9, completion 5, total 14 -- identical in all four cells.
   Assistant text came back as "Hi! How can I" / "Hi there! How can"
   (truncated by max_tokens 5, as intended).

2b. POST /v1/chat/completions with stream:true  -- THE TUTOR'S SHAPE
   200 in all four cells. content-type text/event-stream; charset=utf-8.
   res.body is a ReadableStream in BOTH Chromium and WebKit
   (hasReadableStream true in 4/4), and it was actually drained from page JS:
     chromium hosted : 4 chunks, 2221 bytes, [DONE] sentinel seen
     chromium file   : 5 chunks, 2221 bytes, [DONE] sentinel seen
     webkit   hosted : 5 chunks, 2221 bytes, [DONE] sentinel seen
     webkit   file   : 5 chunks, 2221 bytes, [DONE] sentinel seen
   SSE event shape (keys only):
     { id, object, created, model, service_tier, system_fingerprint,
       choices[], obfuscation }
   Deltas reassembled in-page to the same short reply as 2a. So incremental
   token-by-token rendering works browser-direct on iPhone-shaped WebKit, which
   is the property the tutor needs.
   (Note the `obfuscation` field now present on each SSE chunk; it is padding
   and can be ignored by a consumer.)

3a. POST /v1/audio/speech, model gpt-4o-mini-tts, input "Bonjour",
    response_format "pcm"
   200 in all four cells; bytes readable via res.arrayBuffer().
   content-type: audio/pcm
   byteLength: 69600 / 79200 / 48000 / 124800 across the four cells (the model
   re-synthesises each call, so length varies per call, not per browser).
   NO RIFF header -- first four bytes are non-ASCII sample data, looksLikeRiff
   false. It is raw headerless PCM.
   THE HEADERS SAY NOTHING ABOUT SAMPLE RATE. content-type is the bare
   "audio/pcm" with no rate/channel/bit-depth parameters, and no other visible
   header mentions rate, sample or Hz. A browser client must therefore know the
   format out of band. All four byte counts are exact multiples of 2 and
   consistent with 24000 Hz mono 16-bit (48000 bytes/s): 69600 = 1.45 s,
   79200 = 1.65 s, 48000 = 1.00 s, 124800 = 2.60 s. That rate is confirmed
   independently by 3b.

3b. Same call with response_format "wav"
   200 in all four cells; content-type audio/wav; byteLength 91244 / 84044 /
   64844 / 69644. First four bytes "RIFF".
   WAV header parsed in-page, IDENTICAL in all four cells:
     audioFormat    1      (PCM)
     channels       1      (mono)
     sampleRateHz   24000
     byteRate       48000
     bitsPerSample  16
   So the "pcm" response_format is 24 kHz mono 16-bit little-endian PCM, and
   the only way to learn that from the wire is the wav variant's header -- the
   pcm variant ships no header and no header metadata.

4. WSS wss://api.openai.com/v1/realtime?model=gpt-realtime-mini
   subprotocols ["realtime", "openai-insecure-api-key.sk-****"]  (GA form)
   All four cells identical:
     handshake ACCEPTED, negotiated subprotocol "realtime"
     EVENT TYPE RECEIVED: session.created            <-- the phase 2 assertion
     top-level keys of that event: event_id, session, type
     session object keys: audio, expires_at, id, include, instructions,
       max_output_tokens, model, object, output_modalities, prompt,
       tool_choice, tools, tracing, truncation, type
     then closed by the probe: close code 1000, reason "spike done",
     wasClean true. No error frame, no server-initiated close.
   (No session id, instructions or other values are recorded here, keys only.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CURL CROSS-CHECK -- IS PHASE 1's FINDING AN ERROR-PATH ARTEFACT?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Run off the browser stack against the same origin, valid key read from the
environment, three requests differing only in status class:

  POST /v1/chat/completions, Origin set, VALID key, valid body
    -> HTTP/2 200
       access-control-allow-origin: *
       access-control-expose-headers: X-Request-ID
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray

  POST /v1/chat/completions, Origin set, INVALID key (sk-invalid-spike-key)
    -> HTTP/2 401
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray
       *** NO access-control-allow-origin ***      (phase 1 reproduced exactly)

  POST /v1/chat/completions, Origin set, VALID key, EMPTY body {}
    -> HTTP/2 400
       access-control-allow-origin: *
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray
       access-control-expose-headers: CF-Ray

  POST /v1/audio/speech,          Origin set, VALID key -> 200, ACAO *
  POST /v1/audio/transcriptions,  Origin set, VALID key -> 200, ACAO *

The 400 is the decisive control. It is an authenticated request that fails, and
it still carries ACAO. So the missing header is not a property of "any request
carrying an Authorization header" (phase 1's hypothesis) and not a property of
"any error response" -- it is a property of the AUTHENTICATION-FAILURE response
specifically. The 401-with-a-bad-key path omits ACAO; every other path observed,
success or failure, carries it.

ANSWER: YES. Phase 1's finding was an error-path artefact. It was a correct
observation of the invalid-key 401 and an incorrect generalisation from it.
Phase 1 named this risk itself under "HONEST LIMIT ON THIS CONCLUSION"; phase 2
confirms the limit was the whole of the effect. Everything in phase 1 above the
VERDICT remains factually accurate as a description of the 401 path. The phase 1
VERDICT and its "CONSEQUENCE FOR ROUTE B" are SUPERSEDED by this section.

Practical consequence of the surviving artefact: a browser-direct BYOK client
cannot read the body of a 401. When the user pastes a bad key, fetch() rejects
with an opaque "Failed to fetch" / "Load failed" and the app cannot show
OpenAI's own "Incorrect API key provided" message. Key validation must therefore
be done against GET /v1/models, which DOES return ACAO on its 401 (phase 1,
section 3b) and gives a readable error.code invalid_api_key. Treat an opaque
network failure on the inference endpoints as "probably a bad key" and point the
user at the validation result.

