SPEND
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Approximate, from observed usage:
  chat/completions   11 calls (4 non-stream + 4 stream in-browser, 3 curl)
                     at 9 prompt + 5 completion tokens on gpt-4o-mini   ~ $0.00002
  transcriptions      5 calls x 1.0 s audio on gpt-4o-mini-transcribe   ~ $0.0003
  audio/speech        9 calls x the 7-character input "Bonjour"
                      on gpt-4o-mini-tts (~1-2.6 s audio each)          ~ $0.01
  realtime            4 sessions opened and closed at session.created,
                      no audio sent or received                         ~ $0
  TOTAL                                                                 well under $0.02
Ceiling of $0.05 was not approached.

================================================================================
PHASE 2 VERDICT
================================================================================
BROWSER-DIRECT BYOK IS POSSIBLE for all three REST endpoints -- POST
/v1/audio/transcriptions, POST /v1/chat/completions (both non-streaming and
stream:true), and POST /v1/audio/speech -- from a real browser page carrying the
user's own key in an Authorization header, in desktop Chromium 151 and in
iPhone-shaped WebKit 26.5, from both the hosted https://sotto-steel.vercel.app
origin and a file:// page whose origin is the literal null. Twenty of twenty
endpoint x browser x origin cells returned HTTP 200 with the response body fully
readable from page JS, and every 200 carried Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * plus
Access-Control-Expose-Headers: X-Request-ID, CF-Ray. No relay is required for
any of them. The streaming case is proven end to end and not merely by status:
res.body is a ReadableStream in both engines and was drained chunk by chunk from
page JS to the [DONE] sentinel, so the tutor's incremental rendering works
browser-direct on an iPhone. Speech returns 24 kHz mono 16-bit PCM; the "pcm"
response_format ships raw headerless bytes and NO sample-rate information in any
header (content-type is a bare audio/pcm), so a client must hardcode 24000 Hz --
a fact only obtainable from the "wav" variant, whose RIFF header states
sampleRate 24000, channels 1, bitsPerSample 16, audioFormat 1 (PCM).

The Realtime WebSocket is likewise POSSIBLE and needs no relay: with the GA
subprotocol form ["realtime", "openai-insecure-api-key.<key>"] the handshake was
accepted in all four cells, the negotiated subprotocol was "realtime", and the
first event received was session.created (keys event_id, session, type), after
which the probe closed cleanly with code 1000. The beta subprotocol
"openai-beta.realtime-v1" must NOT be sent; phase 1 showed it is disabled
(beta_api_shape_disabled, close 4000).

Phase 1's REFUSED verdict is WITHDRAWN. It was an error-path artefact: the
absent Access-Control-Allow-Origin is specific to the 401 produced by an invalid
key, and does not occur on 200 responses nor even on a 400 produced by a valid
key with a bad body. The one operational residue is that a bad user key produces
an unreadable opaque failure on the inference endpoints, so key validation
should be performed against GET /v1/models, which returns a readable 401 with
error.code invalid_api_key.

CONSEQUENCE FOR ROUTE B: the escalation condition is NOT met. Route B can be
"the PWA calls api.openai.com directly with the user's key" for transcription,
chat (streaming included) and speech, as well as for Realtime over WSS. B2 is
unblocked and does not need re-scoping around a relay.

REMINDER: the throwaway key used for this run should be revoked now that the
spike is complete.

